1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:12,540
It helped spawn life on Earth, sculpted our landscape,

2
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:13,940
and destroyed the Titanic.

3
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,660
We bag it, manicure it, and use it

4
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:24,160
as the world's most massive lens.

5
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,960
Without it, we'd perish.

6
00:00:26,961 --> 00:00:30,960
Yet it could someday help extinguish us.

7
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,960
It's time to chill with ice on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:52,960
Here's a riddle for you.

9
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,460
What vehicle produced in this factory in Paramount, California

10
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:03,200
has been described as a cross between a street cleaner,

11
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,900
riding lawnmower, and a brick?

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:07,900
Need a hint?

13
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:15,420
It costs as much as a Porsche 911 and tops out at 15 miles

14
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:16,220
per hour.

15
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:22,420
It also lays claim to be the most popular machine in sports.

16
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:32,800
Say hello to that endearing icon of the rink proudly bearing its

17
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:38,800
inventor's name, the Zamboni Ice Resurfacer.

18
00:01:38,801 --> 00:01:52,801
Whenever the ice becomes blemished with gouges, pits,

19
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:55,660
and scrapes.

20
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,640
Count on this lumbering wonder to clean and smooth it.

21
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,940
The machine's key component, located at its rear,

22
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,640
is called the conditioner.

23
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:09,540
On the underside of the conditioner,

24
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:10,920
we have a blade mounted.

25
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:13,440
And it's very sharp, sharp as a razor.

26
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:16,520
And that razor-sharp blade takes a light, thin coat off

27
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:20,720
of the ice, maybe a 16th or a 32nd of an inch of ice.

28
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:24,160
And that snow that's created is brought to the ice

29
00:02:24,161 --> 00:02:25,500
the horizontal auger.

30
00:02:25,501 --> 00:02:31,160
The auger is a screw that actually moves the snow to the center, and that brings the snow

31
00:02:31,161 --> 00:02:36,780
to a paddle which throws it into a vertical auger, another screw that lifts the snow up

32
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:41,400
and throws it into the snow tank.

33
00:02:41,401 --> 00:02:45,780
One of the things you really have to watch out for is the snow plugging up between the

34
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:46,780
conveyors.

35
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:51,780
and you want to push this down, and that pushes the snow away

36
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:55,780
from the two conveyors to unplug any snow that might be

37
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,720
plugging in down there.

38
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:01,620
Following the shaving and snow removal operation,

39
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:03,540
we have a wash water system.

40
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:06,940
In that, we have wash water coming right out of this pipe

41
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,740
into the conditioning unit in front of a squeegee.

42
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:14,280
And the squeegee actually pushes the water forward on the ice

43
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:18,280
and gets any dirt and debris that might be in the ice surface.

44
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:21,520
And that is sucked up with this water pump

45
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:26,120
and recirculated in the wash tank.

46
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,360
The machine completes its resurfacing task

47
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:33,900
by applying a layer of water, typically warm to 140 degrees.

48
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,100
Freezing quickly, it doesn't melt the underlying ice.

49
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,080
But it freezes with fewer air bubbles

50
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:44,280
compared to cooler water, making the final sheet of ice

51
00:03:44,281 --> 00:03:47,680
smoother. Before the first of the Zamboni

52
00:03:47,681 --> 00:03:52,020
machines took to the ice six decades ago, restoring a rink surface

53
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:58,320
was a time-consuming headache. After a tractor pulled a scraper

54
00:03:58,321 --> 00:04:03,040
over the ice, workers scooped up the shavings, sprayed on water,

55
00:04:03,041 --> 00:04:06,840
and allowed it to freeze. The job took an hour and a half

56
00:04:06,841 --> 00:04:11,880
to complete. In the 1940s, refrigeration expert

57
00:04:11,881 --> 00:04:15,880
figured there had to be a better way.

58
00:04:15,881 --> 00:04:19,880
After a few fits and starts, he constructed a primitive prototype

59
00:04:19,881 --> 00:04:22,880
he later called the Model A. My father developed this

60
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:25,880
machine at the end of World War II, and he took advantage of

61
00:04:25,881 --> 00:04:28,880
many of the war surplus parts that were available at that

62
00:04:28,881 --> 00:04:30,880
time. He got the engine from a war

63
00:04:30,881 --> 00:04:33,880
surplus Jeep. He got the axles, which drive the

64
00:04:33,881 --> 00:04:36,880
machine on the eyes, from Army trucks.

65
00:04:36,881 --> 00:04:40,880
And in the rear to lift the conditioner off the eyes,

66
00:04:40,881 --> 00:04:44,880
he got a war surplus cylinder that was used on a Douglas

67
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:48,380
aircraft bomber that was used to lift the landing gear up

68
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:50,820
and down.

69
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:53,620
In the early 50s, Zamboni designed and built

70
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:57,940
ever-improving models, mounting them atop Jeeps.

71
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:00,840
Renowned figure skater Sonia Henney was one of the first to

72
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,540
purchase one for her touring ice show.

73
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,880
In 1954, Zamboni introduced his Model E,

74
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:14,480
the first design he was able to mass produce.

75
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:17,220
This is the last model he built on a Jeep body.

76
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:21,120
He was able to take a real Jeep off the street,

77
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:23,760
bring the controls to the back end of the machine,

78
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:25,760
which were easy to handle.

79
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:30,000
It had a smaller snow tank, but a much larger water system,

80
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,140
and it was a big advance over the earlier machines.

81
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:40,540
In the decades to come, Frank

82
00:05:40,541 --> 00:05:45,380
refined his brainchild. And today, more than 8,000

83
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:49,980
Zamboni machines toiled dutifully in over 60 nations.

84
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:04,300
From our sparkling ice rinks to the top of the world, the solid

85
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:08,200
form of life's precious elixir glistens before us as a roguish

86
00:06:08,201 --> 00:06:15,200
curiosity. We think of ice as a low-temperature

87
00:06:15,201 --> 00:06:23,640
entity, the epitome of cold. But in its own way, ice is hot.

88
00:06:23,641 --> 00:06:26,680
Other materials that cover the Earth's surface, like dirt,

89
00:06:26,681 --> 00:06:30,180
rocks, all that kind of stuff, are pretty far from their

90
00:06:30,181 --> 00:06:33,400
melting point, thousands of degrees.

91
00:06:33,401 --> 00:06:40,400
Even in the coldest winter, ice is still very close to its melting point.

92
00:06:40,401 --> 00:06:43,400
And yet it is still very strong.

93
00:06:43,401 --> 00:06:49,400
You can picture steel within 2 or 3 or 5 degrees of its melting point,

94
00:06:49,401 --> 00:06:53,400
and it would basically be very, very deformable, not very strong.

95
00:06:53,401 --> 00:06:57,400
It wouldn't hold its shape because it's just about ready to melt.

96
00:06:57,401 --> 00:07:01,400
Whereas ice can be within 2 or 3 degrees of its melting point,

97
00:07:01,401 --> 00:07:03,700
And you can land an airplane on it in Antarctica.

98
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:05,100
You can drive across it.

99
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:08,240
You can use it for all sorts of structural purposes

100
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:09,640
because it has quite a bit of strength.

101
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:16,040
Ice shows off another of its unusual properties

102
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:17,440
every time we see it float.

103
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:23,160
Virtually all other solids sink in their liquid form

104
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:27,060
because freezing makes them denser.

105
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:29,560
But water expands.

106
00:07:29,561 --> 00:07:33,560
When it freezes, its molecules link in hexagonal formations,

107
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:36,560
keeping them at a greater distance compared to those in

108
00:07:36,561 --> 00:07:40,560
the liquid state. The more open structure makes

109
00:07:40,561 --> 00:07:46,560
ice 9% less dense than water. Take away this most peculiar

110
00:07:46,561 --> 00:07:49,560
property of ice, and the consequences could be

111
00:07:49,561 --> 00:07:54,560
catastrophic. If ice were more dense than

112
00:07:54,561 --> 00:07:59,560
than liquid water, the ice would sink to the bottom of a lake

113
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,960
and displace the volume of available liquid water.

114
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:07,140
This is really important because as you make more and more

115
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:10,780
of a volume that's unavailable to living things,

116
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:12,740
they have nowhere to go.

117
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:14,220
Nothing could survive.

118
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:19,180
So the fact that ice is less dense than liquid water

119
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:22,260
enables life to carry on even when temperatures shift

120
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,020
and get cold during the winter.

121
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,160
Ice's ability to float is just one of the ways

122
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:29,560
it's made life possible.

123
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:37,300
Nearly 4 billion years ago, extraterrestrial ice borne

124
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:41,640
by comets began bombarding Earth, delivering as much as half

125
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:44,040
of our vast volume of water.

126
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:48,080
Most of the ices would survive until the impact itself,

127
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:51,020
in which case most of them would be vaporized immediately.

128
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,720
And so there would be release of water vapor

129
00:08:53,721 --> 00:08:57,720
life on Earth needs liquid water to form,

130
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:00,400
and it needs carbon-based molecules to form.

131
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:03,000
And comets have an abundance of both.

132
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:05,860
So in a very real sense, we owe our existence

133
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:08,660
to cometary ices and the water that they provide.

134
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:21,840
It's likely that comets punctuated the evolutionary process.

135
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:27,320
It took out the dinosaurs 65 million years ago

136
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:29,360
and allowed the mammals, who were burrowing

137
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:33,060
the creatures at the time, to arise into what we are.

138
00:09:33,160 --> 00:09:35,700
And so we not only owe our existence to comets,

139
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,540
we owe our position atop the world's food chain.

140
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:46,000
In addition to making life possible on Earth,

141
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:50,040
ice has sculpted our planet during ice ages that spanned

142
00:09:50,041 --> 00:09:55,540
that spanned 10% of Earth's 4.5 billion year lifetime.

143
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:59,080
The sculptors were titanic glaciers and ice sheets

144
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:01,480
dominating the landscape.

145
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:06,560
A glacier is basically snow that has fallen and not melted.

146
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:12,160
So years and years and centuries of accumulation of snowfall

147
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:17,540
creates this big pile that compresses under its own way

148
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,040
into ice.

149
00:10:20,041 --> 00:10:23,380
Glaciers move as the extreme pressure of the overlying layers

150
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:28,880
deforms the ice below, causing it to become plastic and flow.

151
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:31,920
Typically, a glacier creeps between 10 and 1,000 feet

152
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:36,020
per year, sculpting whatever earth and rock lies in its way.

153
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:45,500
Perhaps nowhere has ice crafted the earth more majestically

154
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:47,400
than in California's Yosemite Valley.

155
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:52,940
Beginning more than a million years ago,

156
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:55,680
a succession of glaciers followed the path of the ancient

157
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,940
Merced River, which at the time flowed through a valley

158
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,580
featuring gently sloping hills.

159
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:07,600
The ice tore through and removed ton upon ton of the valley's soil

160
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:10,100
and underlying bedrock.

161
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:12,400
It doesn't happen in 1,000 years.

162
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,140
It doesn't happen sometimes in 10,000 years.

163
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:19,340
It's like a sculptor, but sculpting using very slow,

164
00:11:19,341 --> 00:11:23,180
movements, and we don't see it at the time, but generations

165
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:26,140
later, you can see the effect of that movement, and that's the

166
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:29,480
beauty of the valley. The glaciers that carved

167
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:32,520
Yosemite had a critical assist from the granite through which

168
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:36,360
they advanced. Etched in the rock were geologic lines of

169
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:41,460
weakness, now studied by experts like Professor Freka Harmsen.

170
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:45,440
The gouges made it the ideal medium for the ice to sculpt.

171
00:11:45,441 --> 00:11:52,200
These are joints in the granite which are fractures that were caused by stress due to

172
00:11:52,201 --> 00:11:57,680
the uplift of these mountains and also the release of pressure due to the removal of

173
00:11:57,681 --> 00:11:59,880
overlying rock material.

174
00:11:59,881 --> 00:12:05,720
These joints allow the ice to literally penetrate and grab a hold of the granite material and

175
00:12:05,721 --> 00:12:07,340
transport it away.

176
00:12:07,341 --> 00:12:10,240
And we call this glacial plucking.

177
00:12:10,241 --> 00:12:14,540
And the joints, because they're lines of weakness, they determine the resulting shape when a

178
00:12:14,541 --> 00:12:17,340
is moving through. The final product that you end up with

179
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:21,640
is a result of the areas that are less resistant to ice

180
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:25,180
passing through. So the shape of the valley that it is today

181
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:28,480
was already imprinted in the granite yesterday.

182
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:31,500
And all the ice did was release what was already there.

183
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:36,500
As Yosemite's great sheets of ice

184
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:40,100
crafted their masterpiece, they left striking telltale signs

185
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:41,800
of their advance and retreat.

186
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:47,780
Immense boulders, now curiously out of place,

187
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:52,180
litter the bedrock high above the valley floor.

188
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:55,180
This is a classic example of an erratic.

189
00:12:55,280 --> 00:12:58,580
An erratic is a boulder that was transported and deposited

190
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:00,080
by glacial ice.

191
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:02,200
It's actually a different composition

192
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:04,200
than the bedrock down below.

193
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:08,100
It probably came from tens of kilometers toward the east,

194
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:12,700
and was carried downslope by a massive ice sheet,

195
00:13:12,701 --> 00:13:18,100
thousands of feet thick 1 million to 1.3 million years ago.

196
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:22,080
And it was carried down the ancient Mesed River Valley

197
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:26,680
by this glacier, which eventually began to melt and retreat

198
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:31,460
and literally plopped this boulder down on this hillside

199
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:34,660
thousands of feet now above the valley floor in Yosemite.

200
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:40,220
The granddaddy of all glaciers

201
00:13:40,221 --> 00:13:44,060
was the Laurentide Ice Sheet, which grew during the most

202
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:48,220
recent ice age that reached its peak just 18,000 years ago.

203
00:13:51,060 --> 00:13:54,660
As much as two miles thick, the enormous glacier blanketed

204
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:59,280
all of Canada and much of the northern United States.

205
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:01,780
Over the course of 100,000 years,

206
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,880
it left its distinct footprints and deeply influenced

207
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:05,880
our history.

208
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:12,380
Pulverizing the bedrock below it,

209
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:15,620
the Laurentide created the American Midwest's nutrient-rich

210
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:19,060
silt and topsoil that now ranks as some of the most fertile

211
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:19,860
in the world.

212
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:26,460
It enables American farmers to produce more than $200 billion

213
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:29,780
in food every year.

214
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:32,780
The mammoth ice sheet also created America's indispensable

215
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:35,480
inland waterway, the Great Lakes.

216
00:14:35,481 --> 00:14:41,280
It scooped out the soft strata surrounded by firmer bedrock,

217
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:44,680
carving gigantic basins that later filled with the glacier's

218
00:14:44,780 --> 00:14:45,480
meltwater.

219
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:50,720
The lakes now serve as a critical gateway for merchants

220
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:53,940
to transport 110 million tons of goods per year.

221
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:01,580
The Lorentide ice sheet melted away 10,000 years ago,

222
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:04,180
but its effects on the American landscape

223
00:15:04,181 --> 00:15:06,180
reach across the centuries.

224
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:15,620
Today, Americans and ICE remain closely linked,

225
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:17,420
but in an entirely different way.

226
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:23,540
Factories churn it out by the ton to satisfy

227
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:24,840
our insatiable demand.

228
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:34,180
More than any other nation, the United States

229
00:15:34,181 --> 00:15:38,980
United States loves ice. Every day, Americans consume a

230
00:15:38,981 --> 00:15:44,180
whopping 300,000 tons, averaging out to 2 pounds per person.

231
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:51,940
The ice we don't make at our home in our refrigerators is

232
00:15:51,941 --> 00:15:54,120
produced and packaged in hundreds of industrial

233
00:15:54,121 --> 00:15:58,120
facilities, like this one in Vernon, California.

234
00:15:58,121 --> 00:16:01,120
In the wintertime, the demand is relatively small.

235
00:16:01,121 --> 00:16:04,460
We might have one crew working eight hours a day.

236
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:06,260
During the summertime, we're literally

237
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:07,720
working around the clock, where we'll

238
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:10,860
have a few crews going eight-hour shifts, nonstop,

239
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:12,060
trying to keep up with the demand.

240
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:17,580
Union Ice kicks off its production process

241
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:19,840
by pumping chilled, purified water

242
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:22,680
to one of six cylindrical ice makers.

243
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:25,220
Housed within the cylinder chamber

244
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:30,480
are 420 stainless steel tubes that run from the top to the bottom.

245
00:16:30,481 --> 00:16:34,780
Those tubes are sealed off, yet submerged in a liquid ammonia,

246
00:16:34,781 --> 00:16:37,080
which is probably about 10 degrees.

247
00:16:37,081 --> 00:16:39,220
The water is pumped up to the top

248
00:16:39,221 --> 00:16:42,620
and falls down inside the tubes.

249
00:16:42,621 --> 00:16:44,920
So what happens is, with a 10-degree temperature

250
00:16:44,921 --> 00:16:48,140
on its outer surfaces, the water starts to freeze

251
00:16:48,141 --> 00:16:53,340
on the inner walls and starts to freeze from the outside in.

252
00:16:53,341 --> 00:16:55,340
If you've ever wondered why you have a hole in the middle

253
00:16:55,341 --> 00:16:58,580
of your ice cube, it's because it's the passage for the water

254
00:16:58,581 --> 00:17:01,820
to continue to flow down to the bottom.

255
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:03,920
At a certain point in that freezing process,

256
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:06,320
we introduce a hot gas into the chamber,

257
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:09,360
raising the temperature up to about 40 degrees.

258
00:17:09,460 --> 00:17:14,100
The ice starts to sweat and breaks free of the tube

259
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:15,200
falling down.

260
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:18,200
As it's basically sliding down the wall of that tube,

261
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:20,940
there's a cutter at the bottom here that just starts slicing

262
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:22,740
those cubes up into individual pieces.

263
00:17:22,741 --> 00:17:28,240
pieces. The precisely cut cubes

264
00:17:28,241 --> 00:17:31,940
continue on their way, but a crucial step remains before

265
00:17:31,941 --> 00:17:36,080
they're ready to be bagged. When ice is made, there is

266
00:17:36,081 --> 00:17:39,380
moisture that remains on the outer surfaces.

267
00:17:39,381 --> 00:17:44,160
If you were to put that into a bag and freeze it, the individual

268
00:17:44,161 --> 00:17:46,500
pieces would adhere to themselves, and you'd have

269
00:17:46,501 --> 00:17:50,160
like a block in a bag. What we do is we run this moist

270
00:17:50,161 --> 00:17:52,360
through a drying belt.

271
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:55,260
We keep this room at 5 degrees Fahrenheit.

272
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:58,760
20 fans are working, circulating the air,

273
00:17:58,860 --> 00:18:01,400
causing a wind chill factor that takes the temperature down

274
00:18:01,500 --> 00:18:03,200
to minus 10 degrees Fahrenheit.

275
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:08,720
The extreme cold drains water vapor

276
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:12,180
from the chamber, creating an environment so dry

277
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:16,660
it removes all traces of moisture from the cubes.

278
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:20,160
From here, the ice makes its way to the rake room.

279
00:18:20,161 --> 00:18:24,000
where it spills into one of two 200-ton storage bins

280
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:25,460
to await packaging.

281
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:30,700
In each bin is a rake that rotates on a horizontal axis.

282
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:34,700
As ice is being dumped into that particular bin,

283
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:37,820
the rake rises with the ice level.

284
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:41,120
During production, when we need to remove that ice,

285
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:46,120
that rake will rotate, bringing the ice to one end of the bin

286
00:18:46,121 --> 00:18:51,460
it falls down into an auger that conveys the ice into the packaging

287
00:18:51,461 --> 00:18:52,161
room.

288
00:18:58,460 --> 00:19:02,880
2,000 years ago, ice was just as desirable as it is now,

289
00:19:02,881 --> 00:19:07,680
but it had to be harvested from distant snow-capped mountains.

290
00:19:07,681 --> 00:19:11,420
Civilizations including China, Greece, and Rome placed large

291
00:19:11,421 --> 00:19:15,080
amounts of ice and snow in storage pits, where its cooling

292
00:19:15,081 --> 00:19:21,080
effects helped preserve food. The Romans also dropped ice and

293
00:19:21,081 --> 00:19:24,080
snow into their goblets to chill wine.

294
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:31,720
As recently as the 1920s and 30s, the ice we now consume so

295
00:19:31,721 --> 00:19:36,320
ravenously wasn't manufactured, but harvested from frozen ponds

296
00:19:36,321 --> 00:19:41,320
and rivers. This bygone industry endured for more

297
00:19:41,321 --> 00:19:44,920
endured for more than a century and established Americans' love

298
00:19:44,921 --> 00:19:52,920
of, and dependence on, ice. The process developed in New

299
00:19:52,921 --> 00:19:58,920
England in the 1830s was as simple as it was effective.

300
00:19:58,921 --> 00:20:02,120
Step one was to probe the ice with a simple auger.

301
00:20:02,121 --> 00:20:04,920
You check the depth level of the ice to make sure it was safe

302
00:20:04,921 --> 00:20:07,480
for man and beast to go out on the ice.

303
00:20:07,481 --> 00:20:10,520
I like to have the ice at least a foot thick.

304
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:13,280
And you know, you would rotate it like you would any kind of a

305
00:20:13,380 --> 00:20:17,020
drill, and you would cut through the ice, and it would give you

306
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:21,620
a safety level. The harvesting process hit

307
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:25,540
full stride as horses fitted with metal cleats for traction

308
00:20:25,640 --> 00:20:29,140
pulled twin-bladed plows across the ice.

309
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:33,240
The blades etched parallel lines three inches deep and 44 inches

310
00:20:33,241 --> 00:20:40,952
apart. Workers scored row after row until the area to be harvested was striped.

311
00:20:40,953 --> 00:20:41,653
Then,

312
00:20:41,654 --> 00:20:45,640
turning the plows at right angles to the stripes, they created a checkerboard pattern.

313
00:20:45,641 --> 00:20:55,140
Next, workers with saws cut along the grooves, creating uniform blocks that they floated to

314
00:20:55,141 --> 00:21:01,140
ice houses for storage. Ice houses solved the problem of how

315
00:21:01,141 --> 00:21:06,140
to minimize melting decades before the invention of refrigeration.

316
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:08,440
Double walls insulated with sawdust

317
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:11,140
slowed the flow of summer heat into the ice-chilled chambers.

318
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:19,060
The blocks melted at a rate of just seven gallons per hour.

319
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:22,160
150 tons would take six months to become a puddle.

320
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:28,400
By the late 19th century, America's ice harvesters

321
00:21:28,401 --> 00:21:32,400
10 million tons from the nation's frozen waters each winter.

322
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:39,400
In decades to come, their efforts helped establish the ice wagon

323
00:21:39,401 --> 00:21:43,400
and the Iceman as urban icons.

324
00:21:43,401 --> 00:21:46,400
They delivered their blocks to families who used them to preserve

325
00:21:46,401 --> 00:21:49,400
food and cool drinks in sawdust-insulated ice boxes.

326
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:57,400
But the 20th century witnessed the industry's rapid decline.

327
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:03,000
Refrigeration plants began to manufacture ice more efficiently

328
00:22:03,100 --> 00:22:05,700
and year-round.

329
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:09,640
By suspending molds full of water in pre-cooled brine,

330
00:22:09,740 --> 00:22:15,160
these factories could mass produce 25 tons of ice per day.

331
00:22:15,260 --> 00:22:20,760
By the 1950s, refrigerators hummed in 90% of urban and 80%

332
00:22:20,860 --> 00:22:27,200
of rural homes, finally enabling us to create our own ice.

333
00:22:27,201 --> 00:22:34,200
As the ice we manufacture satisfies our needs as consumers,

334
00:22:34,201 --> 00:22:39,200
ice in its raw natural state looms as a potentially deadly

335
00:22:39,201 --> 00:22:40,200
threat.

336
00:22:44,200 --> 00:22:48,200
One enormous mass of ice may be poised to trigger a global

337
00:22:48,201 --> 00:22:49,200
catastrophe.

338
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:03,600
Two great sheets of ice loom as a chilling reminder of Earth's

339
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:05,200
historic big chills.

340
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:13,140
At the South Pole, Antarctica is blanketed by ice covering 5

341
00:23:13,240 --> 00:23:19,560
and 1 half million square miles, an area the size of Brazil.

342
00:23:19,660 --> 00:23:23,360
And near the Arctic, Greenland buckles beneath a sheet as large

343
00:23:23,460 --> 00:23:25,200
as Mexico.

344
00:23:25,201 --> 00:23:28,700
Both immense bodies of ice are more than two miles thick,

345
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:32,200
a burden so heavy that they've depressed the land supporting

346
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:33,200
them below sea level.

347
00:23:40,140 --> 00:23:43,960
Some scientists now suspect that Greenland's ice could exert

348
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:47,060
a different kind of power globally,

349
00:23:47,160 --> 00:23:49,700
triggering a disaster of epic proportions.

350
00:23:49,701 --> 00:23:54,600
portions.

351
00:23:54,700 --> 00:23:57,600
The data suggesting such a scenario emerges from the ice

352
00:23:57,700 --> 00:23:59,340
itself.

353
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:02,940
The reason that the ice is so thick in these places is that

354
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:07,960
there, unlike other places where it snows, a year's snowfall

355
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:09,760
doesn't melt.

356
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:13,060
All of those layers, which represent the snowfall of past

357
00:24:13,160 --> 00:24:15,360
years, are preserved.

358
00:24:15,460 --> 00:24:19,700
And by drilling through these ice sheets and taking samples

359
00:24:19,701 --> 00:24:24,700
of the ice, we can get at these records of Earth's past time.

360
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:27,800
We can get a great deal of information

361
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:30,940
about the Earth's past climate and about the Earth's

362
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:32,940
past atmosphere.

363
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:35,340
It can allow us to be investigators

364
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:39,120
or private eye to take the evidence we see in ice

365
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,820
and learn what happened before we were here.

366
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:44,420
What an amazing archive of data.

367
00:24:44,421 --> 00:24:50,420
Much of this priceless natural archive rests here.

368
00:24:50,421 --> 00:24:55,360
The National Ice Core Laboratory in Lakewood, Colorado,

369
00:24:55,361 --> 00:24:58,860
is the largest repository of ice cores in the world.

370
00:25:01,900 --> 00:25:05,780
The treasure of ice resides in a chamber chilled to 33 degrees

371
00:25:05,781 --> 00:25:07,880
below zero Fahrenheit.

372
00:25:09,780 --> 00:25:12,980
More than 13,000-meter-long tubes holding

373
00:25:12,981 --> 00:25:18,280
tubes hold core segments as old as 400,000 years.

374
00:25:18,380 --> 00:25:21,720
Trapped within the cores are bubbles of air that provide

375
00:25:21,820 --> 00:25:25,920
disturbing and ominous clues to scientists.

376
00:25:26,020 --> 00:25:28,820
They contain the gases present in the atmosphere at the time

377
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:34,440
the layer of snow fell. The samples indicate that during

378
00:25:34,540 --> 00:25:39,140
interglacial eras, like the one we live in now, carbon dioxide

379
00:25:39,141 --> 00:25:42,480
levels held at 280 parts per million.

380
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:45,540
Right now, in this interglacial where it should

381
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:50,080
naturally be about 280 parts per million, it isn't.

382
00:25:50,180 --> 00:25:54,680
We have it elevated by industrial processes up from

383
00:25:54,780 --> 00:26:01,300
the proper 280 up to 360, an elevation of really about a

384
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:02,200
third.

385
00:26:02,300 --> 00:26:06,640
There's no time that we see in the past nearly 1 million

386
00:26:06,641 --> 00:26:10,440
years of record that we have in ice cores when the concentrations

387
00:26:10,540 --> 00:26:14,320
of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases

388
00:26:14,420 --> 00:26:18,120
were anywhere near the high level to which we have it

389
00:26:18,220 --> 00:26:20,360
elevated today.

390
00:26:20,460 --> 00:26:21,480
And that's the problem.

391
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:27,260
Scientists believe increased carbon dioxide levels

392
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:29,300
are powering today's global warming.

393
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:33,500
And that warming may be triggering

394
00:26:33,501 --> 00:26:36,400
a catastrophic acceleration of Greenland's ice

395
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:37,740
across the landmass.

396
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:50,020
Studies indicate that increased surface meltwater

397
00:26:50,120 --> 00:26:53,520
is carving its way down two miles into the ice sheet,

398
00:26:53,620 --> 00:26:57,860
forming wide shafts called moulins.

399
00:26:57,960 --> 00:27:01,020
There's concern that this liquid water

400
00:27:01,021 --> 00:27:05,020
lubricate the glacier where the ice sheets contact the

401
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:11,000
underlying rock and allow it to flow much more rapidly, perhaps

402
00:27:11,100 --> 00:27:16,500
to slide out much more rapidly toward the oceans.

403
00:27:16,600 --> 00:27:20,000
The new discovery worries many.

404
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:23,000
If the entire bulk of the enormous sheet should slide

405
00:27:23,100 --> 00:27:28,920
into the Atlantic, its meltwater could raise sea levels 21 feet.

406
00:27:28,921 --> 00:27:31,960
The rising tide would flood Earth's coastal cities,

407
00:27:32,060 --> 00:27:35,860
including New York, Washington, DC, London, and Tokyo.

408
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:45,540
Experts can't be sure if Greenland's melting ice might

409
00:27:45,640 --> 00:27:49,140
one day ignite such a catastrophe.

410
00:27:49,240 --> 00:27:52,440
But no doubt exists about the clear and present danger ice

411
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:54,140
presents on the high seas today.

412
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:04,520
Since April 14, 1912, when the Titanic met disaster in the

413
00:28:04,521 --> 00:28:08,660
North Atlantic, icebergs have defined our perception of ice

414
00:28:08,661 --> 00:28:10,860
in its untamed natural state.

415
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:17,100
1,503 people perished that fateful night.

416
00:28:17,101 --> 00:28:21,440
And during the century preceding the tragedy, nearly 2,000 others

417
00:28:21,441 --> 00:28:24,980
died in more than 30 ship collisions with North Atlantic

418
00:28:24,981 --> 00:28:25,980
icebergs.

419
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:32,920
An iceberg is a fragment of a glacier that breaks off

420
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:36,220
from a glacier's leading edge into open water.

421
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:39,360
Most of the iceberg is usually way underwater.

422
00:28:39,460 --> 00:28:42,160
You know, the saying the tip of the iceberg is really true

423
00:28:42,260 --> 00:28:44,860
because there's only about 10% of it showing.

424
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:50,580
And what's not showing is the mass of ice beneath that.

425
00:28:50,680 --> 00:28:53,720
Icebergs can be so big that they can't conduct heat

426
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:56,320
quickly through the great distances from their outer

427
00:28:56,321 --> 00:28:59,320
from their outer layers to their cores.

428
00:28:59,420 --> 00:29:04,520
So icebergs may be a good deal colder in their centers

429
00:29:04,620 --> 00:29:08,560
than they are at their edges, where they're newly in contact

430
00:29:08,660 --> 00:29:12,560
with the atmosphere and the oceans.

431
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:14,180
The temperature at an iceberg's core

432
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:17,780
can be as low as 4 degrees below zero Fahrenheit,

433
00:29:17,880 --> 00:29:21,680
making it 10 times harder than the average ice cube.

434
00:29:21,780 --> 00:29:25,080
Icebergs aren't as strong as steel,

435
00:29:25,081 --> 00:29:28,420
But the tragic fate of the Titanic drives home the reality

436
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:30,520
that they can still challenge steel hulls.

437
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:39,620
To understand why, Canadian scientists

438
00:29:39,720 --> 00:29:42,340
are conducting field experiments at sea,

439
00:29:42,440 --> 00:29:44,740
deliberately striking small icebergs

440
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:46,240
and recording the collision data.

441
00:29:46,241 --> 00:29:53,240
of the ocean.

442
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:56,780
Researchers at Canada's Institute of Ocean Technology

443
00:29:56,880 --> 00:30:00,980
have confirmed that the ice at the point of impact may give,

444
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:05,000
but the huge mass of ice surrounding it remains rigid.

445
00:30:05,100 --> 00:30:08,900
This enables an iceberg to focus substantial force and pressure

446
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,700
on the relatively thin steel of a hull,

447
00:30:11,800 --> 00:30:14,600
which can cause it to rupture.

448
00:30:14,601 --> 00:30:18,360
Using iceberg fragments, IoT scientists are trying to learn

449
00:30:18,361 --> 00:30:22,600
more about their physics. They hone each of the fragments

450
00:30:22,601 --> 00:30:28,360
to uniform dimensions for use in a variety of experiments.

451
00:30:28,361 --> 00:30:31,960
In this experiment, a vise-like instrument will rapidly compress

452
00:30:31,961 --> 00:30:36,040
the iceberg sample and measure how much pressure it withstands

453
00:30:36,041 --> 00:30:40,360
before deforming. OK, ready to test.

454
00:30:40,361 --> 00:30:46,600
Testing in 3, 2, 1, now.

455
00:30:46,700 --> 00:30:50,040
The iceberg sample nearly three inches in diameter

456
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:53,400
has withstood a force from the weight of almost a metric ton

457
00:30:53,500 --> 00:30:57,540
before fracturing.

458
00:30:57,640 --> 00:31:00,340
As scientists struggle to unravel the truth about

459
00:31:00,440 --> 00:31:06,260
icebergs, others diffuse the danger they pose by tracking

460
00:31:06,360 --> 00:31:07,260
and monitoring them.

461
00:31:10,360 --> 00:31:14,440
The International Ice Patrol was created in 1913 in response

462
00:31:14,540 --> 00:31:17,100
to the Titanic tragedy.

463
00:31:17,200 --> 00:31:20,700
First with ships and now with radar-equipped planes,

464
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:23,100
it keeps a vigilant eye over the North Atlantic.

465
00:31:26,280 --> 00:31:29,540
Its standard patrol zone covers an area one quarter the size

466
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:33,420
of the continental United States.

467
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:36,220
It's the most dangerous iceberg region frequented heavily

468
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:39,120
by sea traffic.

469
00:31:39,121 --> 00:31:42,560
An average of 500 icebergs drift there from the Arctic

470
00:31:42,660 --> 00:31:46,060
between February and July to threaten the shipping lanes.

471
00:31:50,060 --> 00:31:53,460
Since the ice patrol's inception nearly a century ago,

472
00:31:53,560 --> 00:31:55,880
not one life has been lost due to a collision

473
00:31:55,980 --> 00:31:57,080
in its security zone.

474
00:32:01,180 --> 00:32:04,480
Engineers charged with keeping oil rigs safe from icebergs

475
00:32:04,580 --> 00:32:06,480
have achieved equally impressive results.

476
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:12,360
If an iceberg drifts within 25 miles of any rig

477
00:32:12,460 --> 00:32:14,720
and threatens it, a special vessel

478
00:32:14,820 --> 00:32:17,360
arrives to literally lasso the iceberg

479
00:32:17,460 --> 00:32:19,760
and redirect its course.

480
00:32:19,860 --> 00:32:23,160
Instead of a guy on a horse spinning a loop over his head

481
00:32:23,260 --> 00:32:24,860
and throwing it over the top of the iceberg,

482
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:27,880
what we actually do is we take that rope

483
00:32:27,980 --> 00:32:32,180
and we stream it straight out the stern of the vessel.

484
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:35,420
And he would approach the iceberg, circle the iceberg,

485
00:32:35,421 --> 00:32:38,660
So do a full circle around the iceberg,

486
00:32:38,760 --> 00:32:43,060
come back around, pick up the free end that was trailing

487
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:47,060
initially behind the iceberg, connect up both ends,

488
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:48,660
and then tow it away.

489
00:32:53,280 --> 00:32:55,980
Although icebergs pose a deadly threat,

490
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:58,980
they also inspire wonder.

491
00:32:59,080 --> 00:33:01,440
They once inspired a maverick scientist

492
00:33:01,441 --> 00:33:04,980
to conceive the most daring of war machines.

493
00:33:05,080 --> 00:33:08,280
Imagine an aircraft carrier made of ice.

494
00:33:16,280 --> 00:33:19,600
Nothing on the high seas embodies power more than the

495
00:33:19,700 --> 00:33:21,200
modern aircraft carrier.

496
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:27,440
It girds its might in steel.

497
00:33:31,440 --> 00:33:34,680
More than 60,000 tons of structural steel

498
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:38,980
form the bulk of this vessel, the USS John C. Stennis.

499
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:49,260
Six decades ago, as carriers proved their strategic value

500
00:33:49,360 --> 00:33:53,200
during World War II, the Allies hatched an unusual plan

501
00:33:53,300 --> 00:33:55,600
to build the carrier of all carriers,

502
00:33:55,700 --> 00:33:57,600
not from steel, but ice.

503
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:05,340
The project, codenamed Habakkuk, was the brainchild

504
00:34:05,440 --> 00:34:08,240
of Geoffrey Pike, an eccentric science advisor

505
00:34:08,340 --> 00:34:09,340
for the British High Command.

506
00:34:13,380 --> 00:34:15,380
Pike reasoned that a carrier of ice

507
00:34:15,480 --> 00:34:18,200
would counter the Nazi submarine threat in the Atlantic.

508
00:34:23,500 --> 00:34:26,240
Cargo ships and troop transports were often easy prey

509
00:34:26,340 --> 00:34:29,560
for the German U-boats, as few carriers were available

510
00:34:29,561 --> 00:34:32,160
available to provide air cover over the vast ocean.

511
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:41,900
In 1942, Pike convinced British Prime Minister

512
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:45,200
Winston Churchill that building carriers of ice

513
00:34:45,300 --> 00:34:47,540
could be a fast and inexpensive way

514
00:34:47,640 --> 00:34:50,920
to fill the skies with Allied bombers and fighters.

515
00:34:51,020 --> 00:34:53,120
Many people thought Jeffrey Pike was crazy.

516
00:34:53,220 --> 00:34:55,620
But Jeffrey Pike was no stranger to ice,

517
00:34:55,720 --> 00:34:59,560
and he also knew about the strength of icebergs.

518
00:34:59,561 --> 00:35:01,560
Ice fractures easily, but icebergs don't.

519
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:06,500
No matter how many bombs you hit it with,

520
00:35:06,600 --> 00:35:09,900
no matter how many torpedoes you fire into it,

521
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:13,900
you can't quite get rid of an iceberg.

522
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:15,700
Ice dampens an explosive's energy

523
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:18,420
by preventing the massive and rapid expansion of gases

524
00:35:18,520 --> 00:35:20,120
during detonation.

525
00:35:20,220 --> 00:35:23,660
It'll melt a chunk or a region inside,

526
00:35:23,760 --> 00:35:25,720
but it doesn't actually blow it apart.

527
00:35:25,820 --> 00:35:29,360
It kind of goes, and sort of absorbs that energy

528
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:33,760
energy inside. Pike's chemists added a common

529
00:35:33,761 --> 00:35:37,520
ingredient to ice to produce a hybrid as blast resistant as an

530
00:35:37,521 --> 00:35:39,760
iceberg. Pike and his team discovered that

531
00:35:39,761 --> 00:35:44,400
if you take 14% sawdust or wood pulp by weight added to water,

532
00:35:44,401 --> 00:35:49,760
you come up with a remarkably strong substance. 14% sawdust by

533
00:35:49,761 --> 00:35:55,200
weight with 86% weight by water.

534
00:35:55,201 --> 00:36:04,300
When the mixture was frozen, the wood fibers and the sawdust bound the ice crystals together,

535
00:36:04,301 --> 00:36:08,700
strengthening the ice. So here's what they came up with. This

536
00:36:08,701 --> 00:36:13,142
is pikerete. The name is a combination of pike's name and concrete.

537
00:36:13,143 --> 00:36:14,600
And this is a standard

538
00:36:14,601 --> 00:36:23,040
block ice. So let's see how strong they are.

539
00:36:23,041 --> 00:36:24,500
Now let's try to picrete.

540
00:36:31,980 --> 00:36:35,880
Picrete not only was strong, incredibly strong,

541
00:36:35,881 --> 00:36:38,200
it also was insulated by the wood pulp

542
00:36:38,201 --> 00:36:39,740
so that it wouldn't melt.

543
00:36:39,741 --> 00:36:42,100
Regular ice, if you set it out on a hot, sunny day,

544
00:36:42,101 --> 00:36:44,140
would be gone in the matter of that day.

545
00:36:44,141 --> 00:36:49,080
It would take days for the picrete to melt.

546
00:36:49,081 --> 00:36:52,680
Engineers drew up plans for a colossal picrete carrier,

547
00:36:52,681 --> 00:36:57,180
stretching 2,000 feet long and 300 feet wide.

548
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:00,380
It would displace 2.2 million tons,

549
00:37:00,480 --> 00:37:03,820
26 times that of the largest ship of the era, Britain's

550
00:37:03,920 --> 00:37:07,420
Queen Elizabeth. The pycrete hull and flight

551
00:37:07,520 --> 00:37:11,700
deck would be 40 feet thick and be contained by and insulated

552
00:37:11,800 --> 00:37:15,340
with a wood composite material.

553
00:37:15,440 --> 00:37:18,980
Frigid air forced through a network of cardboard tubes

554
00:37:19,080 --> 00:37:21,240
would keep the pycrete frozen.

555
00:37:22,680 --> 00:37:29,680
In 1943, workers at Patricia Lake in Jasper, Canada,

556
00:37:29,681 --> 00:37:34,680
secretly built a 1 50th scale model of the ship.

557
00:37:34,681 --> 00:37:39,680
It spanned 60 feet and weighed 1,100 tons.

558
00:37:44,680 --> 00:37:48,680
The model functions successfully in tests on Lake Patricia, but

559
00:37:48,681 --> 00:37:55,680
on Lake Patricia, but in 1944, Pike's dream ended here.

560
00:37:55,780 --> 00:38:01,320
The need for Habakkuk evaporated, as the German subs were being

561
00:38:01,420 --> 00:38:05,440
sunk faster than they could be built.

562
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:09,640
Land-based aircraft had gained longer ranges,

563
00:38:09,740 --> 00:38:12,440
and the logistics of building the full-size carrier

564
00:38:12,540 --> 00:38:13,340
proved daunting.

565
00:38:13,341 --> 00:38:18,340
The Allies canceled the project and abandoned the scale model

566
00:38:18,341 --> 00:38:19,340
on the lake.

567
00:38:23,340 --> 00:38:27,340
It finally sank, but only after its unrefrigerated pycrete

568
00:38:27,341 --> 00:38:31,340
endured more than three hot summer months before melting.

569
00:38:31,341 --> 00:38:36,340
All that remains is the debris of its wooden frame.

570
00:38:36,341 --> 00:38:44,720
wooden frame. Today, 60 years after Habakkuk

571
00:38:44,721 --> 00:38:49,460
melted into history, another supersized project using ice is

572
00:38:49,461 --> 00:38:53,220
keeping scientists busy at the South Pole.

573
00:38:53,221 --> 00:38:56,900
The high-tech device they're creating just may solve some of

574
00:38:56,901 --> 00:38:59,900
the universe's most profound mysteries.

575
00:39:06,340 --> 00:39:16,380
For nearly half a century, Antarctica has played host

576
00:39:16,381 --> 00:39:19,880
to scientists eager to conduct experiments in an environment

577
00:39:19,881 --> 00:39:27,900
like nowhere else on Earth. At the Amundsen-Scott South Pole

578
00:39:27,901 --> 00:39:32,500
Station, astronomers are crafting a supersized project using

579
00:39:32,501 --> 00:39:38,240
treasure of ice. They're assembling an enormous

580
00:39:38,241 --> 00:39:41,740
device they hope will unravel secrets about the formation

581
00:39:41,741 --> 00:39:47,340
and nature of the universe. They call their apparatus Ice

582
00:39:47,341 --> 00:39:54,260
Cube, and the name fits. It's a monstrous telescope utilizing

583
00:39:54,261 --> 00:39:58,260
one cubic kilometer of Antarctica's ice.

584
00:39:58,261 --> 00:40:03,260
Comprised of ancient, extremely pure snow, the ice is remarkably

585
00:40:03,360 --> 00:40:04,260
transparent.

586
00:40:04,360 --> 00:40:08,260
If you go down about 800 to 1,000 meters into the ice cap,

587
00:40:08,360 --> 00:40:10,200
you get ice that's as clear as plastic.

588
00:40:10,300 --> 00:40:12,900
Looking at samples of it, you couldn't even tell that there

589
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:14,080
was anything there.

590
00:40:14,180 --> 00:40:16,840
You can see hundreds of feet through this ice, and so that's

591
00:40:16,940 --> 00:40:19,880
exactly the type of ice that we need to do these types of

592
00:40:19,980 --> 00:40:21,580
experiments.

593
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:24,760
IceCube detects subatomic particles called

594
00:40:24,761 --> 00:40:29,000
IceCube detects subatomic particles called neutrinos,

595
00:40:29,100 --> 00:40:31,700
hurtling invisibly all around us.

596
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:35,300
They interact with virtually nothing.

597
00:40:35,400 --> 00:40:37,880
A billion of them have just flown through you in the past

598
00:40:37,980 --> 00:40:39,900
second, and you never felt a thing.

599
00:40:43,920 --> 00:40:49,420
Neutrinos originated billions of years ago in distant galaxies.

600
00:40:49,520 --> 00:40:52,920
They're the product of such cataclysmic events as supernovas,

601
00:40:52,921 --> 00:40:56,620
collisions between black holes, and the annihilation of dark

602
00:40:56,621 --> 00:41:00,720
matter, the invisible and mysterious substance that holds

603
00:41:00,721 --> 00:41:02,720
the universe together.

604
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:09,160
Cable down. Cable down.

605
00:41:09,161 --> 00:41:11,480
By detecting neutrinos with sophisticated

606
00:41:11,481 --> 00:41:15,480
instruments, IceCube can peer back in time and help

607
00:41:15,481 --> 00:41:18,980
reconstruct how our universe began.

608
00:41:18,981 --> 00:41:22,420
And it may help scientists define exactly what dark matter

609
00:41:22,421 --> 00:41:27,720
is, revealing deep secrets about the cosmic glue that binds

610
00:41:27,721 --> 00:41:30,720
everything.

611
00:41:30,721 --> 00:41:34,660
IceCube consists of 4,800 optical sensors the size of

612
00:41:34,661 --> 00:41:36,760
basketballs.

613
00:41:36,761 --> 00:41:41,080
They're suspended on 80 cables, plunging down more than 7,400

614
00:41:41,081 --> 00:41:45,780
feet into Antarctica's thick ice sheet, a distance equal to six

615
00:41:45,781 --> 00:41:49,520
Empire State buildings piled atop one another.

616
00:41:49,521 --> 00:41:52,160
In the dark, frigid silence, the sensors

617
00:41:52,260 --> 00:41:55,200
can detect several hundred neutrinos every day.

618
00:41:55,300 --> 00:41:59,700
About one in a million that streams through our detector

619
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:03,760
actually decides to collide with an atom in the ice.

620
00:42:03,860 --> 00:42:06,700
And when it collides, it makes other particles

621
00:42:06,800 --> 00:42:09,940
that we can see, unlike the neutrino itself,

622
00:42:10,040 --> 00:42:13,040
and that we see through the blue light they radiate

623
00:42:13,140 --> 00:42:14,540
when they stream through the ice.

624
00:42:17,040 --> 00:42:19,320
The transparent ice enables the blue light

625
00:42:19,321 --> 00:42:23,020
to travel visibly for several hundred feet to one of IceCube's

626
00:42:23,021 --> 00:42:26,820
sensors. The sensor can detect its direction

627
00:42:26,821 --> 00:42:30,100
and help scientists backtrack the neutrino to its point of

628
00:42:30,101 --> 00:42:36,140
origin. In doing so, IceCube promises

629
00:42:36,141 --> 00:42:39,780
to help astronomers map previously unknown regions at

630
00:42:39,781 --> 00:42:41,240
the far edge of the universe.

631
00:42:41,241 --> 00:42:49,240
ice. Whether ice is helping to

632
00:42:49,241 --> 00:42:55,280
revolutionize our knowledge, clogging the frigid seas, or

633
00:42:55,281 --> 00:43:03,600
simply clinking in our glasses, it is and defines cool.

634
00:43:03,601 --> 00:43:11,240
It endures as our most powerful frozen asset.

